<http://data.silknow.org/object/7e1b2b18-0736-31ed-a757-f9b5e25d19cf>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "embroidered, 1770-1829, Persian"@en , "White cotton plain weave embroidered with silk in counted thread stitches, edged in buttonhole stitch with an added border of silk net embroidered with silk [lacis] and an added silk fringe.\nThe main part of the scarf is probably Ottoman and could be either from Turkey or from parts of Iran heavily influenced by Ottoman embroidery techniques.  The ground fabric and the reversibility of the stitches is typical of Ottoman work. A series of narrow bands form a border: black diamond-heads, a band of mauve, dark blue and dark pink stars, a wider band with black leaves forming a double meander, a repeat of the stars and diamond-heads down the sides and along both ends. In addition, across the ends is a row of blue cypress trees [?] alternating with a pink or faded purple flower. \nThere is an added lacis border along the lower end only. It has a dark pink silk square net ground and is embroidered with white silk to form a grid of diamonds with a border of blocks.\nA black silk fringe has been added across the bottom and a little way up the sides."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/eef40ab7-c27f-3b67-a7c2-e37074187516> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "White cotton plain weave embroidered with silk in counted thread stitches, edged in buttonhole stitch with an added border of silk net embroidered with silk [lacis] and an added silk fringe.\nThe main part of the scarf is probably Ottoman and could be either from Turkey or from parts of Iran heavily influenced by Ottoman embroidery techniques.  The ground fabric and the reversibility of the stitches is typical of Ottoman work. A series of narrow bands form a border: black diamond-heads, a band of mauve, dark blue and dark pink stars, a wider band with black leaves forming a double meander, a repeat of the stars and diamond-heads down the sides and along both ends. In addition, across the ends is a row of blue cypress trees [?] alternating with a pink or faded purple flower. \nThere is an added lacis border along the lower end only. It has a dark pink silk square net ground and is embroidered with white silk to form a grid of diamonds with a border of blocks.\nA black silk fringe has been added across the bottom and a little way up the sides."@en , "embroidered, 1770-1829, Persian"@en ;
